==English==

===Etymology===
The [[#Adjective|adjective]] is {{etydate|r|1400|1450|nocap=1}}, in {{w|Middle English}}, the [[#Verb|verb]] c. 1513; from {{inh|en|enm|congregat|congregat(e)|t=(''of people'') banded together; (''of liquids'') accumulated; (''of muscles'') contracted; (''of wounds'') closed up}}, borrowed from {{uder|en|la|congregātus}}, {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|congregō||to congregate}} (see {{m|en|-ate|pos=etymology 1, 2 and 3}}, from {{m|la|con-||with, together}} + {{m|la|gregō||to collect into a flock}}, from {{m|la|grex||flock, herd}}. See {{m|en|gregarious}} and {{m|en|egregious}}. Participial usage of the [[#Adjective|adjective]] up until {{w|Early Modern English}}.

