===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To collect into an [[assembly]] or [[assemblage]]; to bring into one place, or into a united body.
#: {{syn|en|amass|assemble|compact|bring together|gather|mass|Thesaurus:round up}}
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws
|passage=Any multitude of Christian men '''congregated''' may be termed by the name of a church.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=7|passage=The great receptacle Of '''congregated''' waters he called Seas.|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Coleridge Aids to Reflection|passage=Cold '''congregates''' all bodies.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=68|volume=II|text=And here I would observe, that love of admiration seems scarcely to be properly appreciated; it is the only bond of society—we could not otherwise endure each other. It is the true source of the sublime, and, my conscience obliges me to add, of the ridiculous. Still, it is the strong necessity of admiring each other, and the being admired in our turn, that has built cities, '''congregated''' multitudes, and organised what we call our present state of civilisation.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come together; to assemble; to [[meet]].
#: {{syn|en|assemble|begather|forgather|Thesaurus:assemble}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|I|iii|passage=Even there where merchants most do '''congregate'''.}}

